1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,437
And to act on it, you first have to measure heat the way a body does.

2
00:00:04,387 --> 00:00:06,737
A thermometer reads the air.

3
00:00:06,737 --> 00:00:10,750
But a body on a sunlit plaza feels something else entirely.

4
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:20,375
The pavement, the walls, the sky all radiate heat onto your skin — and that radiant load, not the air, is the biggest driver of daytime discomfort.

5
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:26,512
So CoolSpend judges every plan on UTCI, the Universal Thermal Climate Index.

6
00:00:26,512 --> 00:00:35,400
UTCI folds four things into one number in degrees Celsius: air temperature, radiant heat off hot surfaces, wind, and humidity.

7
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:39,350
It is, in short, what a body actually feels.

8
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:44,237
Above twenty-six degrees, comfort tips into moderate heat stress.

9
00:00:44,237 --> 00:00:52,212
We evaluate it where it matters — a July day, nine to five, at one-point-one metres, the height of a walking person.

10
00:00:52,212 --> 00:00:56,462
And here is the lever: shade attacks the radiant term directly.

11
00:00:56,462 --> 00:00:59,725
Cool the surfaces, and you cool the person.

